# Office lock files — created while a .pptx/.docx is open, never content
~$*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- docs/plans
#
# QA renders. Checking a deck's layout means converting it to PDF and then to
# one JPEG per slide, and the pptx thumbnail script writes a labelled grid
# beside it. All three are throwaway, all three land next to the deck, and all
# three are binary — so committing them means a new blob per QA pass forever.
docs/plans/*.pdf
docs/plans/slide-*.jpg
docs/plans/slide-*.png
docs/plans/*thumbnails*.jpg
docs/plans/*-thumbs*.jpg

# THE .pptx FILES ARE NOT IGNORED, and that is deliberate.
#
# Three of the five are generated — build-phase-2, build-phase-3 and
# build-summary each write theirs — so ignoring those looks obviously right.
# It is not: endless-phase-0-v3.pptx and endless-phase-1.pptx have NO builder.
# They were hand-built before deckkit existed, and git is the only copy.
# Ignoring *.pptx would untrack them, and the next clean checkout would simply
# not have them.
#
# Tracking three decks and ignoring two is worse than either, so all five stay.
# If someone writes the two missing builders, revisit this.

# OS cruft
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Python bytecode. deckkit is imported by the deck builders, so running one
# leaves a .pyc behind; one was committed.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Node
node_modules/
sim/run-log.json
tools/generated/

# Simulation caller keys. The pattern here was `sim/.keys-*.json` — with a
# hyphen — and the file the simulator actually writes is `sim/.keys.json`.
# It did not match, so six live API keys were committed in 47d9d20 and would
# have gone to a public repo on the first push. A near-miss caused by one
# character, which is the usual shape of this mistake.
sim/.keys*.json

# Credentials. Never committed — this repo has otherwise had no stored secrets
# at all, and an API key in git history cannot be taken back out.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
